Q: PHP, $regex to obtain each URL of videos about $pattern A: <h2>Why don't use a regular expression?</h2>
<p>With a regex, I would first suggest using the <strong>x</strong> flag for the
e<strong>x</strong>tended notation with spaces and comments and the <strong>i</strong> flag for
case-<strong>i</strong>nsensitive:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>/
\s                    # href should be preceded by a space.
href=&quot;(?&lt;href&gt;[^&quot;]+)&quot; # Capture the href value (⚠️ simple solution).
[^&gt;]*                 # Any other attribute after href.
&gt;                     # Closing of opening &lt;a&gt; tag.
(?&lt;link_text&gt;         # Capture the text of the link.
  [^&lt;]*?              # Anything before &quot;Greta&quot;.
  \bGreta\b           # The word &quot;Greta&quot;.
  [^&lt;]*               # Anything after &quot;Greta&quot;.
)
/gix
</code></pre>
<p>I'm capturing the <code>href</code> value and the link text in two named groups. This
can be done with the <code>(?&lt;group_name&gt; ... )</code> syntax. The regex may look
complicated for a beginner, but it's rather simple and clearly not elaborated
enough to handle special cases.</p>
<h3>Problem 1 : syntax of attributes</h3>
<p>Notice that <code>href=&quot;...&quot;</code> could also be <code>href = &quot;...&quot;</code> or <code>href='...'</code>, which
I don't take into consideration, to simplify my answer. You can improve it
a bit, by using this kind of regex to read attributes:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>/
# A space, href, and then optional spaces around the equal sign.
\shref\s*=\s*
(?:
  # Option A: double-quotes around the value.
  &quot;(?&lt;href_1&gt; [^&quot;]* )&quot;
|
  # Option B: single-quotes around the value.
  '(?&lt;href_2&gt; [^']* )'
|
  # Option C: no quotes. Value is non-space chars.
  (?&lt;href_3&gt; [^\s&gt;]* )
)
/gix
</code></pre>
<p>See it in action here: <a href="https://regex101.com/r/8VKVxO/1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://regex101.com/r/8VKVxO/1</a></p>
<p>It's a bit stupid, because you have 3 different capturing groups and you
have to read the 3 of them. Or you could
<a href="https://regex101.com/r/8VKVxO/2" rel="nofollow noreferrer">capture the 3 of them in a parent group</a>
and then trim it to remove the double or single quotes:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php $href = trim($match['href'], '&quot;\''); ?&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Ok, let's leave this improvement for the moment and go on with the rest of
the link parsing:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>You may have some other attributes after the <code>href</code> attribute, so take it
in consideration with <code>[^&gt;]*</code> to match any char not being the closing tag.</p>
</li>
<li><p>For &quot;Greta&quot; itself, use the word boundary <code>\b</code> before and after, if
not you could match something like &quot;gretard&quot;, which wouldn't be what you
are looking for.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>You can test the simplified regex here: <a href="https://regex101.com/r/9wYKYK/1" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://regex101.com/r/9wYKYK/1</a></p>
<p>In PHP, it kind of works also, because the HTML is simple and as expected:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

$data = &lt;&lt;&lt;DATA
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;Greta Thunberg talk about ...&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;another video of Greta Thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM&quot;&gt;Israel and Greta Thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another video&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another video&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;...'
DATA;

$regex = &lt;&lt;&lt;REGEX
/
href=&quot;(?&lt;href&gt;[^&quot;]+)&quot; # Capture the href value.
[^&gt;]*                 # Any other attribute after href.
&gt;                     # Closing of opening &lt;a&gt; tag.
(?&lt;link_text&gt;         # Capture the text of the link.
  [^&lt;]*?              # Anything before &quot;Greta&quot;.
  \bGreta\b           # The word &quot;Greta&quot;.
  [^&lt;]*               # Anything after &quot;Greta&quot;.
)
/ix
REGEX;

preg_match_all($regex, $data, $matches, PREG_SET_ORDER);

print_r($matches);
</code></pre>
<p>Run it here: <a href="https://onlinephp.io/c/87b952" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://onlinephp.io/c/87b952</a></p>
<h3>Problem 2 : The link may have child elements</h3>
<p>Now, <strong>forget the regex solution</strong> because it will <strong>never</strong> handle all
the possible cases. Typically, what happens if the link text contains some
child elements like this?</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;a href=&quot;...&quot;&gt;Talk from &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Greta&lt;/em&gt; Thunberg&lt;/strong&gt; about ...&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>
<h3>Problem 3 : images or malformed HTML</h3>
<p>And what happens if we have an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or some malformed HTML, such as a
missing closing tag, or some <code>&lt;</code> or <code>&gt;</code> characters in an attribute
(where it normally shouldn't)?</p>
<p>You'll just fiddle more and more the regular expression and
finally will be re-creating an HTML parser yourself! ⚠️☠️</p>
<h2>Solution : Using a DOM parser</h2>
<p>In PHP, we can use <em>DOMDocument</em> and the <em>XPath</em> query feature, to find
all the <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> tags containing a specific text within some rather crappy
but valid HTML code (attributes with double or simple quotes, or none,
and spaces around the &quot;=&quot; sign).</p>
<p>We still have to check that &quot;<em>Greta</em>&quot; is a word, and not part of another
word, like &quot;<em>Gretard</em>&quot;, &quot;<em>Hogreta</em>&quot; or &quot;<em>Saint-Greta</em>&quot;. This can't be
directly done via <em>XPath</em> and can be checked with <em>PHP</em>, while reading
the results of the query. We can, again, use a regular expression for
this purpose:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

$data = &lt;&lt;&lt;DATA
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Greta Thunberg talk...&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href = 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok'&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href ='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok'&gt;Video of &lt;em&gt;greta&lt;/em&gt; thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok'&gt;Not Gretard Thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&gt;Greta should be fine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href =  &quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM&quot;&gt;Oh! Israel and Greta&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;Don't match Hogreta&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another video&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLswKLWw&quot;&gt;No Saint-Greta, please&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
...
DATA;

$dom = new DOMDocument();
// Suppress warnings for malformed HTML
libxml_use_internal_errors(true);
$dom-&gt;loadHTML($data);
libxml_clear_errors();

$xpath = new DOMXPath($dom);

// The word to search, in lowercase.
$word_to_search = 'greta';
// The same word to search, in uppercase. Used in the translate() function of
// the XPath query, later below.
$word_to_search_uppercase = strtoupper($word_to_search);
// A regular expression, used as security, to match &quot;greta&quot; as a word only.
// Some names are composed, such as &quot;Jean-Claude&quot;. Unfortunately, \b matches
// word boundaries when the adjacent char (before or after) is not a letter
// or a number, which is not totally correct in our case. And this may also
// not work correctly with some other languages, using accents and other
// non-ascii letters. So we'll use a negative lookbehind and a negative
// lookahead, to avoid having a letter \p{L}, a number \p{N} or a hyphen.
// We'll also enable the &quot;u&quot; = unicode flag, to handle all languages.
$word_escaped = preg_quote($word_to_search);
$regex_word_to_search = &lt;&lt;&lt;REGEX
/
(?&lt;![\p{L}\p{N}-])    # No letter, number or hyphen before.
$word_escaped         # The word to search (greta).
(?![\p{L}\p{N}-])     # No letter, number or hyphen after.
/xiu
REGEX;

// Let's use a XPath query to get all &lt;a&gt; tags with a text containing
// the word to search in lowercase, after having transformed all
// uppercase letters of this word into the corresponding lowercase letter.
// In XPath 2.0, you could use the lower-case() function instead of this
// use of the translate() function. Don't use the text() function as it
// will only return the first text node and not all the text content
// of the lin (if it has some child elements like &lt;em&gt;, &lt;span&gt;, etc).
$query = &lt;&lt;&lt;XPATH_QUERY
//a[contains(
    translate(., '$word_to_search_uppercase', '$word_to_search'), 
    '$word_to_search'
)]
XPATH_QUERY;

echo &quot;XPath query:\n$query\n\n&quot;;

$links = $xpath-&gt;query($query);
$urls = [];

echo &quot;XPath results:\n&quot;;
foreach ($links as $link) {
    // Display the found link and href value.
    echo &quot;Found: &quot; . $link-&gt;nodeValue . &quot;\thref: &quot; . $link-&gt;getAttribute('href');
    // Check that it's really a word and not part of a word.
    if (preg_match($regex_word_to_search, $link-&gt;nodeValue)) {
        echo &quot;\tOK\n&quot;;
        // Store the URL and link title in a dictionnary, in case we have
        // several times the same href value within different links.
        $urls[$link-&gt;getAttribute('href')][] = $link-&gt;nodeValue;
    } else {
        echo &quot;\tNot a word!\n&quot;;
    }
}
echo &quot;\n&quot;;

echo &quot;\$urls = &quot; . var_export($urls, true) . &quot;\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>Run it live here: <a href="https://onlinephp.io/c/4a5ee3" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://onlinephp.io/c/4a5ee3</a></p>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>XPath query:
//a[contains(
    translate(., 'GRETA', 'greta'), 
    'greta'
)]

XPath results:
Found: Greta Thunberg talk...   href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok   OK
Found: Video of greta thunberg  href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok   OK
Found: Not Gretard Thunberg href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok   Not a word!
Found: Greta should be fine href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok   OK
Found: Oh! Israel and Greta href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM   OK
Found: Don't match Hogreta  href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok   Not a word!
Found: No Saint-Greta, please   href: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLswKLWw   Not a word!

$urls = array (
  'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok' =&gt; 
  array (
    0 =&gt; 'Greta Thunberg talk...',
    1 =&gt; 'Video of greta thunberg',
  ),
  'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok' =&gt; 
  array (
    0 =&gt; 'Greta should be fine',
  ),
  'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM' =&gt; 
  array (
    0 =&gt; 'Oh! Israel and Greta',
  ),
)
</code></pre>
<h3>Improving it with PHP custom functions in XPath queries</h3>
<p>As <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/2255089/casimir-et-hippolyte">Casimir et Hippolyte</a> proposed, it's possible to add custom functions to <em>XPath</em> queries
to directly get the nodes we want without having to do the second filtering
step by looping through the results :</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

$data = &lt;&lt;&lt;DATA
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Greta Thunberg talk...&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href = 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok'&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href ='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok'&gt;Video of &lt;em&gt;greta&lt;/em&gt; thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href='https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TMrtLsQbaok'&gt;Not Gretard Thunberg&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another title&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&gt;Greta should be fine&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href =  &quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qJv-upsvMfM&quot;&gt;Oh! Israel and Greta&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;Don't match Hogreta&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLsQbaok&quot;&gt;some another video&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TzrtLswKLWw&quot;&gt;No Saint-Greta, please&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
...
DATA;

$dom = new DOMDocument();
// Suppress warnings for malformed HTML
libxml_use_internal_errors(true);
$dom-&gt;loadHTML($data);
libxml_clear_errors();

$xpath = new DOMXPath($dom);
// Register the php: namespace (required)
$xpath-&gt;registerNamespace(&quot;php&quot;, &quot;http://php.net/xpath&quot;);

/**
 * Custom filter for XPath.
 * 
 * @param String $linkText The text we want to check.
 * @param String $word The word you are looking for.
 */
function hasWord(string $linkText, string $word) {
    global $regex_word_to_search;
    return (bool)preg_match(
        '/(?&lt;![\p{Xan}-])' . preg_quote($word) . '(?![\p{Xan}-])/iu',
        $linkText
    );
}

// Let's add our filter to match only our desired word.
// Depending on PHP version, you can't use the key =&gt; callable syntax, so this
// is why I declared the function above and just passed its name.
$xpath-&gt;registerPhpFunctions(['hasWord']);

// The XPath query to get all &lt;a&gt; tags using our custom filter.
// It's important to use the dot and not the text() function, because
// we want the string equivalent of all the text nodes merged together.
$query = '//a[php:function(&quot;hasWord&quot;, string(.), &quot;Greta&quot;)]';

echo &quot;XPath query:\n$query\n\n&quot;;

$links = $xpath-&gt;query($query);
$urls = [];

echo &quot;XPath results:\n&quot;;
foreach ($links as $link) {
    // Store the URL and link title in a dictionnary, in case we have
    // several times the same href value within different links.
    $urls[$link-&gt;getAttribute('href')][] = $link-&gt;nodeValue;
}

echo &quot;\$urls = &quot; . var_export($urls, true) . &quot;\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>You can test this version here: <a href="https://onlinephp.io/c/e8b7b0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://onlinephp.io/c/e8b7b0</a></p>

